Draw the **Gloamfin**, a 128x128 **sprite sheet** of a deep-sea enemy, using only
the drawing tool.

The Gloamfin is **the Listener**: a **non-playable pursuer** in a deep-sea
maze-chase game — an eyeless predator that hunts the player by sound. You are
drawing the *enemy*, never the player.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw` binary already on your `PATH`. It is
the sole channel: you build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a time**,
and the ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is
the **authoritative output that is scored**, not the pixels on disk. An image
produced any other way (writing a PNG directly, editing `canvas.png` by hand) is
discarded and flagged; only the recorded operations count.

After each call the binary re-renders the current image to `canvas.png` and
appends the operation to `actions.json`. A blank starting canvas and an empty log
are already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each call applies one operation passed as JSON:

```
draw apply --json '{"op":"fill_circle","cx":20,"cy":16,"r":8,"color":"#c46bff"}'
```

Read `schemas/operations.json` for the exact operations and their parameters, and
**read `canvas.png` after your calls** to see your progress and decide what to do
next.

